Audited in a `cp -a` copy at scratch/x8/repo (kept .git). All commands below
were run there against a real render, not template source.

## 1. Template integrity (heading-repair verification)

- Independent tag-balance check (open/close counts for h1-h6/div/section/span,
  after stripping HTML/Go-template comments) across all 50 manifest templates
  incl. the 6 hand-repaired files (budget-analysis, failure-points,
  historical-backtest, monte-carlo, spending-phases, social-security):
  ALL BALANCED. Two apparent mismatches were false positives from tag names
  mentioned inside `<!-- -->` and `//` JS comments (accounts_card.html,
  accounts.html), confirmed by direct inspection, not real markup.
- `go build ./...`: clean. `go test -count=1 ./internal/templates/...
  ./web/... ./internal/handlers/whatif/...`: all `ok`.
- Built cmd/server, ran it against a throwaway `BUDGET_DATA_DIR` (empty temp
  dir, no seed data needed — pages render empty-state at 200 OK) and curled
  /dashboard /whatif /insights /transfers /major-expenses. Parsed each
  response with a strict Python html.parser tag-stack tracker (not the
  template source):
  - dashboard: 1 h1, sequence h1,h2×5 — no skip, 0 tag-mismatch errors.
  - insights: 1 h1, sequence h1,h2,h2,h2,h2,h3,h3,h2 — no skip, 0 errors.
  - major-expenses: 1 h1, sequence h1,h2,h3,h2 — no skip, 0 errors.
  - transfers: 1 h1, sequence h1,h2,h2 — no skip, 0 errors.
  - whatif: 1 h1, 28 more headings (all the promoted whatif/* partials
    concatenated) — no skip anywhere, 0 tag-mismatch errors. This directly
    exercises all 6 hand-repaired files in one render; the repair is proven
    complete, not just claimed.
  - Landmarks: every page has exactly one <main>, one <nav>, one <header>
    (banner) and one <footer>; transfers has a second, non-landmark <header>
    nested inside <main> wrapping just the h1/intro text — byte-identical to
    master (`git diff master -- web/templates/pages/transfers.html` shows no
    header/h1 hunk), not an X8 defect.

## 2. Contrast spot-audit (18 combinations computed from the actual shipped
   CSS values, `git diff master -- web/static/css/tailwind.css` confirms the
   RGB/alpha figures used came straight from web/static/css/tailwind.css)

PASS (light and dark):
- green-700/white 5.02:1, green-700/green-50 4.79:1 (green-600/white was
  3.30:1 — confirms why the sweep was necessary)
- gray-600 captions on white 7.56:1, gray-50 7.23:1, rose-50 6.88:1,
  indigo-50 6.76:1, amber-50 7.29:1, emerald-50 7.17:1
- verdict-bar band label/value colors, both themes, all 4 Health states
  (read from render.go's verdictClasses map), band bg composited correctly
  from the shipped rgba() alpha values over the real body background
  (bg-gray-900 dark / bg-gray-50 light): emerald-700/emerald-50 5.21:1,
  amber-700/amber-50 4.84:1, rose-700/rose-50 5.72:1, gray-600(label)/
  gray-700(value) on gray-50 7.23/9.86:1; dark: emerald-300/400, amber-300/
  400, rose-300/400, gray-400/200 on their composited band backgrounds all
  6.24-11.86:1.
- svg exemption (monte-carlo.html:268): byte-identical to master
  (`git diff master` confirms), non-text icon, out of scope for the sweep as
  documented. (Missing aria-hidden on this decorative svg is PRE-EXISTING —
  see Observations.)

FAIL — dark-mode contrast, ACCESSIBILITY.md points 7 and 12 (WCAG 1.4.3):
  The task's own list of "8 hand-picked tint-background exceptions" in
  kpis.html + guardrails.html (plus 4 more instances of the same pattern,
  `text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-500`, in the same two files) leave
  `dark:text-gray-500` (#6b7280) as caption/label text against dark
  translucent or solid card backgrounds that are much darker than the
  gray-800 the shade was calibrated for. Computed straight from the shipped
  CSS (`rgba(136,19,55,.2)` etc. composited over the real body
  `bg-gray-900`/card `bg-gray-800`, per `git diff master -- ...` confirming
  none of these background classes changed):

    kpis.html:77,81   (bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800, solid)      3.04:1
    kpis.html:111,113 (bg-rose-50 dark:bg-rose-900/20)          3.48:1
    kpis.html:179,185 (neutral: bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800)    3.04:1
    guardrails.html:6,72 (bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 card, solid) 3.04:1
    guardrails.html:26,33 (bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/20)         2.96:1
    guardrails.html:46,53 (bg-green-50 dark:bg-green-900/20)     2.80:1

  All 12 sites are text-xs/text-sm/text-[10px] — none qualify as "large
  text" — so the 4.5:1 threshold applies; every one fails it, several by a
  wide margin. `git diff master` shows these `dark:*` class tokens were left
  unchanged while the paired light-mode class WAS swept on the same line
  (e.g. kpis.html: `text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500` (master) →
  `text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-500` (X8) at line 111) — i.e. this is not
  inherited baggage the sweep walked past, it is the sweep's own documented,
  reviewed decision to leave these 8(+4) alone, and that decision is
  measurably wrong: the light side got fixed, dark did not, so dark mode is
  no longer at parity with light (point 12) on these captions.

